Title: Supply a correctly namespaced trustInfo in node.exe's manifest
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

  This application has failed to start because its side-by-side
  configuration is incorrect.

On XP, from a node.exe that starts normally on Vista, 7 and 10. Nothing is
wrong with the imports by this point; the image never gets that far. The
manifest is what XP rejects, and the two binaries say why:

  electron.exe (starts on XP)  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
  node.exe     (does not)      <trustInfo>

trustInfo is an asm.v3 element. With no xmlns of its own it inherits the
asm.v1 default from the enclosing <assembly>, where no such element exists.
Vista's side by side engine onwards skips what it does not recognise, so the
defect is invisible everywhere but the one place it matters: XP fails the
manifest outright and will not load the image.

Node does not write that element. The linker does, and lld writes it without
the namespace -- createDefaultXml in lld/COFF/DriverUtils.cpp emits a literal
"<trustInfo>" when /MANIFESTUAC is on, which it is by default. MSVC's link.exe
has always written the asm.v3 form, which is why this has gone unnoticed; it
is an lld bug and worth fixing there, a one line change to that string.

So this does both halves. src/res/node.exe.extra.manifest, which is already
merged into every executable's manifest by the VCManifestTool settings in
node.gypi, gains a correct trustInfo; and the linker is told not to write its
own, because two trustInfo elements in different namespaces is not something
the merge can resolve and would leave XP rejecting the manifest regardless.

/MANIFESTUAC:NO changes no policy: asInvoker with uiAccess false is what the
linker's default requests and what the manifest now states in its place. The
<compatibility> block beside it needs no change -- its namespace is unknown to
XP as well, but it sits in an element XP does not recognise and so is skipped
rather than rejected, which is exactly how electron.exe gets away with the
same block.

diff --git a/node.gypi b/node.gypi
index 6587270c..be8932ff 100644
--- a/node.gypi
+++ b/node.gypi
@@ -38,7 +38,21 @@
         'VCManifestTool': {
           'EmbedManifest': 'true',
           'AdditionalManifestFiles': 'src/res/node.exe.extra.manifest'
-        }
+        },
+        'VCLinkerTool': {
+          # /MANIFESTUAC:NO. Not a change of policy -- asInvoker with no UI
+          # access is what the default asks for and what the extra manifest
+          # above now states instead. This only stops the linker generating
+          # its own copy, because the one it generates is malformed: lld's
+          # createDefaultXml writes a bare <trustInfo>, with no xmlns, and
+          # that element belongs to asm.v3. Merged under an asm.v1 <assembly>
+          # it names an element no schema has. Vista and later ignore it; XP
+          # fails the manifest and the image will not load at all. Leaving
+          # both in place would be worse than either alone, since two
+          # trustInfo elements in different namespaces is not something the
+          # merge can resolve.
+          'EnableUAC': 'false',
+        },
       },
     }],
     [ 'node_shared=="true"', {
diff --git a/src/res/node.exe.extra.manifest b/src/res/node.exe.extra.manifest
index e2e9f175..3700d1ba 100644
--- a/src/res/node.exe.extra.manifest
+++ b/src/res/node.exe.extra.manifest
@@ -1,5 +1,21 @@
 <?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8' standalone='yes'?>
 <assembly xmlns='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1' manifestVersion='1.0'>
+  <!-- Supplied here rather than left to the linker, which emits this element
+       with no xmlns of its own. trustInfo is an asm.v3 element, so without one
+       it inherits the asm.v1 default from <assembly> above and names an
+       element that does not exist in that namespace. Every Windows from Vista
+       on ignores the stray element and starts the process anyway; XP's side by
+       side engine rejects the whole manifest and refuses to load the image
+       with "the application has failed to start because its side-by-side
+       configuration is incorrect". See the EnableUAC note in node.gypi for
+       how the linker is kept from emitting its version alongside this one. -->
+  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
+    <security>
+      <requestedPrivileges>
+        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false"/>
+      </requestedPrivileges>
+    </security>
+  </trustInfo>
   <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
     <application>
       <!-- Windows 10 -->
